course: dictionary
lesson: 2565
title: Delighted and its many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Delighted means very pleased and happy. «She was delighted with her present» tells you she loved her gift. Look at the words after it, and you will see the feeling it describes.
explain: Delighted is also the past form of the verb delight. «The show delighted the children» means the show gave the children great pleasure. Here the word is an action, not just a feeling.
explain: Grown-ups use delighted in polite speech too. «I'd be delighted to help» is a warm way of saying yes, gladly. Writers say absolutely delighted, not very delighted, so use absolutely when you want to add strength.
fact: Delighted means very pleased and happy.
fact: Delighted is also the past form of the verb delight: delight, delights, delighted, delighting.
fact: «Delighted» can mean gave great pleasure to someone, as in «The show delighted the children».
fact: In polite speech, «I'd be delighted to help» means I would be very willing to help.
fact: Common phrases are delighted with, delighted to, delighted that and delighted by.
exercise: «She was delighted with her present.» What does delighted mean here? | Very angry | Very cold | *Very pleased and happy | Very tired
exercise: «The show delighted the children.» What does delighted mean in this sentence? | Left the children behind | Took the children home | Made the children cross | *Gave great pleasure to the children
exercise: Owen says, «I'd be delighted to help.» What does delighted suggest? | *Very willing, happy to say yes | Very upset about the job | Sure to forget the job | Hardly able to help at all
test: «So it's not magic, it's practice!» said Owen, delighted. How does Owen feel? | Frightened | Confused | *Happy and pleased | Sad
test: «His mum is delighted: feeding a hungry child is her favourite job.» What does delighted tell us? | She is tired of cooking | Her mum is angry about cooking | *She is very happy about it | She is hungry too
exam: Which sentence uses delighted to mean very willing? | Owen was delighted. | *I'd be delighted to help. | She was delighted with her present. | The show delighted the children.
